{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The entire commandment.</b> If you began a commandment, finish it completely, and so they said: one who begins a commandment — they say to him: 'complete it,' which means finish it. And anyone who begins a commandment and another finishes it, it is called by the name of the one who finishes it. Whence do we know this? From Moses, who began the commandment of Joseph's ark, as it is said: ״ויקח משה את עצמות יוסף״, and he did not finish it, for he did not bring them into the land — rather, Israel brought him in. Therefore it is called by their name, as it is said: ״ואת עצמות יוסף אשר העלו בני ישראל ממצרים קברו בשכם״, and it is not written אשר העלה משה. And further, Rabbi Yannai said: anyone who begins a commandment and does not finish it buries his wife and his sons. Whence do we know this? From Judah, as it is said: ״מה בצע כי נהרוג את אחינו״ — they sat to break the bread and to recite the blessing of ha-motzi. He said to them: we are killing our brother and blessing and breaking — cursed be HaShem! Rather, come let us sell him to the Ishmaelites. And it is written: ״וישמעו אחיו״, and he did not finish the commandment to save him completely. Therefore it is written: ״ותמת בת שוע אשת יהודה״, and Er and Onan his sons. Therefore, when a person begins a commandment, he should finish it entirely. Therefore it is written: ״כל המצוה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:38.991743051Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}